import Bitcoin.Numeric.Amount
import Bitcoin.Numeric.Interface

/-!
# BitcoinProofs.Property.Y05 — Amount/fee property corpus (Review-4, contributor Y05)

Generated by `scratch/Y05/reference.py` (`python3 reference.py` regenerates
this file byte-for-byte; all pseudo-random cases use `random.Random(20261016)`),
so every numeral below is a PYTHON verdict and the `decide` proofs force the
Lean spec (frozen W04 module `Bitcoin.Numeric`) to recompute the same value.
Ground truth: Bitcoin Core @ cce32b16874e85e8a00cfa1a2190f0b6f4dcaf66 —
  * consensus/amount.h:14-27      (CAmount, COIN, MAX_MONEY, MoneyRange)
  * consensus/tx_check.cpp:31-43  (output accumulation, CVE-2010-5139)
  * consensus/tx_verify.cpp:170-220 (input accumulation, fee, belowout)
  * primitives/transaction.cpp    (GetValueOut, throw-on-overflow)
  * validation.cpp:2518,2540-2541 (block fee accumulator, BLK-091)

Sections:
  1. MoneyRange boundary grid (int64 edges + MAX_MONEY edges)
  2. feeOf vectors (edges + seeded random + belowout)
  3. checkedSum = CheckTxInputs input accumulator (TXV-024 class)
  4. End-to-end fee instances (valueIn - valueOut vs python)
  5. Block fee accumulator verdicts (validation.cpp:2540-2541 / BLK-091;
     `feeAccumInRange f = MoneyRange f` by rfl, core/Bitcoin/Block/Connect.lean:166)
  6. int64 wraparound boundaries (CVE-2010-5137 class)
  7. Named theorem instances (axiom-audited in scratch/Y05/axioms.lean)

Known model-fidelity divergence pinned below: Lean `checkedSum` checks the
cumulative range only, while Core's accumulators PAIR it with a per-element
check (tx_check.cpp:37-39, tx_verify.cpp:192). On lists containing
out-of-range elements whose prefix sums stay in range ([10,-5] etc.) Lean
accepts where Core rejects — findings file spec/db/findings-w4/Y05.md,
F-Y05-001. Equivalence holds under the per-element MoneyRange hypothesis.
-/

